+//! Where cold records live when they are not on this box: a port, a rule, and the two passes that
+//! carry it out.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.21-cold-tiering`. Every step before this one treated the store as a place on one
+//! machine: `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery` replays its log, `lakelet/4.8-compaction` merges its
+//! segments, `lakelet/4.9-retention` decides what leaves for ever, `lakelet/4.19-backup-restore`
+//! moves the whole thing somewhere else. None of them has asked the question a store that is
+//! filling the disk it was given has to ask: the box is expensive, most of these segments are never
+//! read again, and **the bytes of a cold segment do not have to be on it**. This module is the
+//! answer, and the answer is three things rather than a filesystem trick: a *port* that says what a
+//! tier is, a *rule* that says which segments are cold, and two passes that move them out
+//! ([`sweep`]) and bring them back ([`hydrate`]).
+//!
+//! # The port is blobs, not segments
+//!
+//! [`Objects`] is `put`, `get`, `delete` and a listing that pages. Four calls, and every one of
+//! them is about **whole blobs under a key** — nothing in the port knows what a segment is, what a
+//! manifest is, or that this crate exists. That is the point of a port: [`filesystem`] is one
+//! implementation (a directory, and the one this course's own specs run against), [`s3`] is another
+//! (an S3-shaped endpoint over the client in [`http`]), and a deployment may have a third. What the
+//! port buys is the thing a store cannot get any other way: the tiering rule, the two passes and
+//! the safety argument written **once**, and run against every implementation
+//! (`docs/authoring.md`'s conformance gate, and `conformance.toml` at the project's root: this
+//! step's spec is run once per backend it declares).
+//!
+//! Absence is a value: `get` answers `None` for a key nobody wrote rather than an error, because
+//! "that blob is not in the tier" is the ordinary case of `hydrate` and the only honest answer to
+//! a question about something that was never put there. `delete` is idempotent for the same
+//! reason — a pass that deletes what is already gone has not failed — and it is safe to *repeat*
+//! for a third one: deleting the same key twice leaves the same tier.
+//!
+//! # The listing is the authority, and it pages
+//!
+//! A caller with a port must be able to answer "what is in the tier under this prefix" **without**
+//! asking the box's own disk, and [`Objects::list`] is that answer: keys in a deterministic order,
+//! one page at a time, with a [`Cursor`] naming where the next page starts. [`listing`] is the loop
+//! that walks the pages — it lives here, and not in a caller, because "the endpoint said there is
+//! more" is a fact only the loop can act on (`lakelet/4.21`'s page counts it, and the S3-shaped
+//! stub answers a page **shorter than the `max-keys` it was asked for**, which is the shape a
+//! first implementation gets wrong: it stops at the end of the first page and reports a store that
+//! is missing half its objects).
+//!
+//! A cursor is a key rather than an offset, and the reason is that the tier changes while it is
+//! walked: an offset counts what a prefix held when the walk began, so a key that arrives in the
+//! middle of a walk shifts every page after it, and a key that leaves makes the next page skip one.
+//! A key is a position that means the same thing whatever else happened.
+//!
+//! # The ordering, which is the whole safety argument
+//!
+//! [`sweep`] puts the object **first** and removes the local file **second**:
+//!
+//! | crash point | what is on the device | is the store still readable? |
+//! | ----------- | --------------------- | --------------------------- |
+//! | before the `put` returns | the segment's file, where it was | yes, unchanged |
+//! | between the `put` and the delete | the file **and** the object, the same bytes | yes — the reader reads the local file |
+//! | after the delete | the object | through [`hydrate`], before anything reads the segment |
+//!
+//! The middle row is the property: a crash in the window leaves the segment in *both* places, and
+//! the two are the same bytes because the `put` is what put them there. Nothing is ever in neither
+//! place, which is what the other order — delete, then `put` — would give a crash one instruction
+//! later: a manifest that names a segment whose bytes are in neither place, which is not a cold
+//! store, it is a lost one.
+//!
+//! The keys make a retried `put` safe as well. A segment's key is a function of its identity and
+//! the port's own prefix ([`object_key`]), so **the key is the idempotency key**: a `put` that was
+//! sent and answered into a lost connection, and sent again, writes the same object, and the second
+//! one is not a second copy. That is also why an S3-shaped implementation may retry a `put` at all
+//! (`http::ATTEMPTS`) — a request that can be repeated without changing the outcome is a request a
+//! client may repeat, and one whose key carried an instant or a counter would not be.
+//!
+//! # [`hydrate`] is how the store's readers stay untouched
+//!
+//! `lakelet/4.8-compaction` reads a **directory**: the manifest names segments, the files are there,
+//! and a segment whose file is not the length the manifest states is refused rather than read. This
+//! step does not change any of that — it cannot, and `lakelet/4.9-retention` is where the file that
+//! would have to change is pinned by an earlier spec: `segments.manifest` keeps its layout and its
+//! version byte. So the tier writes the bytes of a cold segment **back into the file the manifest
+//! already names**, checks the length it wrote against the manifest's number, and refuses — leaving
+//! no file behind — when the object it fetched is not that length. Hydration is what makes a
+//! directory agree with the manifest again, and it is why nothing in the engine's read path knows
+//! this module exists.
+//!
+//! A store whose cold segments are out of the box is therefore **not openable** by a process
+//! without a tier: `Segments::open` refuses it and names the file that is not there. That is the
+//! honest answer rather than a hole — a store that answered something from a manifest naming
+//! segments nobody has is a store whose answers are missing records — and it is the one thing to
+//! weigh before sweeping: the tier is for the process that owns the store (which holds the manifest
+//! in memory and hydrates the segment it is about to read), not for a stranger with `ls`.
+//!
+//! # The tier holds no manifest of its own
+//!
+//! What the tier holds is answered by [`Objects::list`], and **nothing here is written durably**:
+//! no index, no map from ordinal to key, no epoch. The alternative was a second manifest, and the
+//! argument against it is the one above turned around: a file that says what is in the tier is a
+//! file that can disagree with the tier, and a store that trusted it would refuse to open for a
+//! reason nobody could see — or worse, open and answer a hole. A tier that lost its listing is a
+//! tier that holds nothing, which is a truth a caller can act on (`hydrate` refuses, naming the key
+//! it needed, and the operator restores the bucket).
+//!
+//! Two smaller consequences are deliberate:
+//!
+//! * **the sidecar indexes are not tiered.** `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`'s files are derived state
+//!   — a store without one answers the same questions having read more — and a tier that carried
+//!   them would be carrying a second copy of something that can be built again. A store that was
+//!   swept and hydrated may find an index beside a segment that was away; that is a *stale* index
+//!   for the same bytes, and `lakelet/4.14` reads a stale index as precision lost, never as a
+//!   record lost;
+//! * **the log is not tiered either.** `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s file is a prefix that a restart
+//!   replays, and what a segment holds is already derived from it; moving bytes out of the log
+//!   would mean rewriting the file a crash's readability is defined by. `lakelet/4.20-capacity-model`
+//!   measured what that costs (`log_share_permille`), and the honest sentence is that this step
+//!   makes the *segments* cheap and leaves the log where it is.
+//!
+//! # What this module does not do
+//!
+//! It reads no clock: [`Policy`] is applied at a `now` a caller hands in, and the S3-shaped
+//! implementation dates its signatures from a value in its [`s3::Config`] rather than from the
+//! machine, so two runs of the suite sign the same bytes (`lakelet/4.16-profiling` made the same
+//! decision about measurement, and `lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr` about flushing). It does not decide
+//! *when* a pass runs — [`sweep`] and [`hydrate`] are one pass each, for a task that decides the
+//! schedule, exactly as `Segments::compact` and `Segments::expire` are. And it does not delete
+//! anything the tier holds: `delete` is called for a key a `put` just wrote only by a caller that
+//! has decided to undo it, which nothing here does.
+//!
+//! One more thing is deliberately missing and worth naming: nothing here counts a *tier's* handles
+//! or bytes. `lakelet/4.17-soak` counts what a store holds open, and the files this module opens
+//! through [`Open`] are charged to the store that owns them — the segment file a `sweep` reads and
+//! the file a `hydrate` writes are the store's own. The connection an S3-shaped tier holds open is
+//! not counted, because it is not a file of the store's, and a soak that watched this module would
+//! need a counter of its own: that is a later step's business, not a silent hole here.
+
+pub mod filesystem;
+pub mod http;
+pub mod s3;
+
+pub use filesystem::Directory;
+pub use s3::{Bucket, Config};
+
+use std::fmt;
+use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
+
+use crate::engine::compaction::{self, SegmentInfo, Segments};
+use crate::engine::soak::Open;
+
+/// How many keys a listing asks for in one page when its caller does not say.
+///
+/// The default is the number `ListObjectsV2` answers with when a caller names no `max-keys`, and it
+/// is a *default* rather than a rule: a page size is the caller's decision (it is what `max-keys`
+/// means on the wire), and both implementations here take one
+/// ([`Directory::with_page`](filesystem::Directory::with_page),
+/// [`Bucket::with_page`](s3::Bucket::with_page)). This step's own suite asks for pages of two keys,
+/// because a listing that pages cannot be judged on a store that fits in one page.
+pub const PAGE_KEYS: usize = 1000;
+
+/// One blob's name in the tier, as a page hands it back.
+///
+/// A cursor is a key and not an offset: see the module docs. It is opaque to a caller — the port
+/// promises one thing about it, which is that handing a page's `next` back asks for the page after
+/// that one, and nothing else is promised, because an S3-shaped endpoint hands back a token of its
+/// own invention (a base64 of the last key, in the implementation this course's stub speaks).
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Cursor(String);
+
+impl Cursor {
+    /// A cursor naming `key`: where the next page of a listing starts.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn new(key: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
+        Self(key.into())
+    }
+
+    /// The text of this cursor, for a port that has to put it in a request.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str {
+        &self.0
+    }
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for Cursor {
