+//! An S3-shaped endpoint as a tier: SigV4, the five operations, and the page token.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.21-cold-tiering`. [`super::filesystem`] is a tier on the same box as the store;
+//! this is the one that is not. It is the second implementation the port exists for, and it is the
+//! half of this step that is at **design depth** for `outbound/http-client`: `relay/2.7`'s client
+//! established a request, a deadline and an answer, and what a *signed* request adds is the part
+//! that cannot be got wrong quietly —
+//!
+//! * **the canonical request is built from the request that is sent.** The signature is computed
+//!   over [`http::Request::canonical_request`], which reads the method, the path, the query pairs,
+//!   the signed headers and a payload hash taken from the body *of that value*, and the bytes that
+//!   leave the socket are rendered from the same value ([`http`]). There is no second assembly of
+//!   the same request anywhere in this file, which is the only way "the string that is signed
+//!   cannot disagree with the bytes that are sent" is a property rather than a hope — and a
+//!   disagreement is a `403` whose message says the signature did not match, with nothing in it
+//!   about which of the fifteen lines differed;
+//! * **the instant is a value.** `x-amz-date`, the credential scope's date stamp and the signature
+//!   all come from [`Config::date`], which a caller builds. Nothing here reads a clock, so two
+//!   machines sign the same bytes for the same request — the same rule `lakelet/4.16-profiling`
+//!   applied to measurement and `lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr` to flushing, and here it is what makes
+//!   a pinned signature vector a test rather than a flake;
+//! * **the key is the idempotency key.** `put` is a `PUT` to an object whose name is a function of
+//!   the segment's identity ([`super::object_key`]), so a `put` that was sent twice wrote one
+//!   object; that is what [`http::ATTEMPTS`] is allowed to repeat, and it is the reason a tier's
+//!   retry is safe while a delivery's retry (`relay/2.8-retry-budget`) needs a whole budget;
+//! * **a listing is followed by its token.** `ListObjectsV2` answers at most `max-keys` keys and
+//!   says whether it is truncated; the page a caller gets is one request's worth, and the token is
+//!   what the *next* request carries. The loop that follows those tokens is
+//!   [`super::listing`](super::listing), deliberately not here: a directory's pages follow the same
+//!   rule, and a loop that lives in one implementation is a loop the other one's cases cannot judge
+//!   (an endpoint is allowed to answer a page *shorter* than the `max-keys` it was asked for, which
+//!   this course's stub does on purpose);
+//! * **a `403` is read, not retried.** It is the one refusal an operator has to act on, and the two
+//!   usual causes are a scope that does not match the endpoint's (region, service, date) and a
+//!   credential that is not the one the bucket knows. [`Error::Refused`]'s message says so, and
